-### Pure Random-Access Decompression
-**Definition:** A strategy where data decompression is performed each time the renderer needs to access a single voxel, transmitting data in compressed form from CPU to GPU [S2].
-**Steps:** Transmit compressed data from CPU to GPU [S2]. Perform on-demand, fast, and spatially independent decompression for each requested voxel [S2].
-**Pitfall:** GPU implementations of random-access (e.g., VQ, HVQ) are costly in performance and rarely implement multisampling or high-quality shading without deferred filtering [S2].
-**Comparison:** Unlike partial decompression, this method never fully decompresses the dataset in GPU memory, better exploiting memory bandwidth during transmission [S2].
+### [Pure Random-Access Decompression](5361-pure-random-access-decompression.md)
+Pure Random-Access Decompression is a compression-domain rendering strategy where volumetric data is transmitted to the GPU in compressed form and decompressed on-demand for each individual voxel access during the rendering process. Unlike methods that decompress a full working set into GPU memory before rendering, this approach never fully decompresses the dataset in GPU memory, thereby better exploiting memory bandwidth during transmission. It is distinguished from [Compression Domain Rendering](960-compression-domain-rendering.md) by its lack of pre-fetching or block-based reconstruction; instead, it relies on immediate, spatially independent decompression for every requested sample.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The per-fragment cost involves multiple texture fetches (index, address, two codebooks, color table) [S1, unofficial]. The total time is proportional to the number of fragments and the cost of dependent texture lookups [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The index texture is $1/4$ the size of the original data in every dimension, resulting in a significant reduction in memory footprint compared to uncompressed data [S1, unofficial]. The codebook textures and address texture add overhead but are generally small relative to the full dataset [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** The method suffers a performance loss of a factor of 2-3 compared to rendering uncompressed data [S1, unofficial]. This is attributed to the computational cost of the on-chip decoding process [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Minimizes data transfer between main memory and GPU memory [S1, unofficial]. Leverages GPU texture hardware for parallel reconstruction [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Performance overhead compared to uncompressed rendering [S1, unofficial]. Requires careful handling of texture coordinates and slicing to ensure correct reconstruction of the original data blocks [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance**: The simultaneous decoding and rendering of compressed data sets results in a loss in performance of about a factor of 2-3 compared to the rendering of uncompressed data [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory**: The index set is 1/4 the original size in every dimension [S1, unofficial]. The address texture is periodically repeated to avoid storing a full-sized address map [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths**: Hardware accelerated 3D texture mapping allows for parallel reconstruction [S1, unofficial]. Minimizes data transfer between main memory and GPU memory [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations**: Performance loss compared to uncompressed rendering [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method addresses the challenge of rendering massive volumetric datasets by keeping data in compressed format at all stages except during transient local reconstruction [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the Deferred Filtering Architecture for Compressed Volumes, which coordinates visibility- and resolution-aware adaptive data loading with GPU-accelerated per-frame data decompression, deblocking, and rendering [S1, unofficial]. Not given in the sources.
+Compression Domain Rendering was introduced in a 2003 paper by researchers at the Technical University of Munich (TUM) [S1, unofficial]. It utilizes a hierarchical quantization scheme and hardware-accelerated texture mapping to perform on-chip decoding [S1, unofficial].
 
